# The reader's serial port: a name that survives re-enumeration, owned by the service's group.
#
# Install to /etc/udev/rules.d/99-splitforge-reader.rules — see docs/deployment.md.
#
# SYMLINK: a USB serial port is ttyUSB0 until it re-enumerates while the old node is still
# held open, and then it is ttyUSB1. A pulled cable does exactly that to a process that has not
# yet noticed. --serial names /dev/splitforge-reader, which follows the device.
#
# GROUP and MODE: the node is otherwise root:dialout 0660, and the service account is not in
# dialout. Adding it would grant every serial port on the device, not just the reader's.
#
# 99: after 50-udev-default.rules, which gives every tty to dialout.
#
# idVendor and idProduct: the reader is SparkFun's M7E Hecto board (ADR-0035), whose USB-UART
# bridge is a CH340C. 1a86:7523 is the CH340 in the kernel's ch341 driver table. These come from
# documentation, not from a board: confirm them with `udevadm info /dev/ttyUSB0` when one is
# plugged in. So the rule matches the bridge, not the module behind it, and any other CH340
# adapter on the same Pi contends for the name, as would a GPS whose NMEA arrived through one.
#
# No ATTRS{serial}: CH340-family bridges are not expected to carry a USB serial number, so two
# identical boards cannot be told apart here. Two readers on one Pi would need a rule per
# physical USB port (KERNELS, or ENV{ID_PATH}) instead. See docs/readers/thingmagic-m7e-hecto.md.
#
# KERNEL names the same device group as the unit's DeviceAllow=char-ttyUSB. A bridge that
# enumerates as ttyACM needs both changed, and apps/splitforge-edge/tests/unit_file.rs fails
# until they agree.
SUBSYSTEM=="tty", KERNEL=="ttyUSB[0-9]*", ATTRS{idVendor}=="1a86", ATTRS{idProduct}=="7523", SYMLINK+="splitforge-reader", GROUP="splitforge", MODE="0660"
